  "body": "## Motivation\n\nStyling buttons differently based on activation likelihood is an [established UX best practice](https://www.nngroup.com/articles/button-states-communicate-interaction/#toc-button-states-vs-button-styles-3)\nThis involves giving primary buttons more visual weight and tertiary buttons less to reduce end-user cognitive overhead.\n\nAdditionally, another established UX best practice is to style destructive actions differently as an additional visual cue information scent.\n\n<img width=\"431\" height=\"196\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/cd1321b1-a484-4619-827e-71853d9662cd\" />\n\nCurrently, class-based design systems use ad-hoc classes like `btn-primary`, `btn-secondary` etc as styling hooks, whereas component-based design systems use dedicated attributes like `variant`, `priority` etc. \nOften there is no semantic primitive, and the priority is encoded through its presentation, e.g `<button class=\"btn-outline\">`. Some are mixed (e.g. `variant=\"danger\"`\n\nSome examples below. For design systems with only presentational knobs, I used attributes to nudge towards something like this:\n\n| Button component | Primary | Secondary | Tertiary | Danger |\n|--------|-------|------|------|------|\n[Carbon Design System Button](https://carbondesignsystem.com/components/button/usage/) | - | `kind=\"secondary\"` | `kind=\"tertiary\"` | `kind=\"danger\"` |\n[Spectrum Button](https://opensource.adobe.com/spectrum-web-components/components/button/) | `variant=\"primary\"` | `variant=\"secondary\"` | - | `variant=\"negative\"` |\n[GitHub Primer Button](https://primer.style/product/components/button/) | `variant=\"primary\"` | `variant=\"default\"` | `variant=\"invisible\"` | `variant=\"danger\"` |\n[ShadCN Button](https://ui.shadcn.com/docs/components/button) | - | `variant=\"secondary\"` | `variant=\"ghost\"` | `variant=\"destructive\"` |\n[Web Awesome Button](https://webawesome.com/docs/components/button) | - | `appearance=\"outlined\"` | `appearance=\"plain\"` | `variant=\"danger\"` |\n[Taiga UI Button](https://taiga-ui.dev/components/button) | `appearance=primary` | `appearance=secondary` | `appearance=flat` | `appearance=flat tuiAppearanceMode=\"invalid\"` |\n[Basecoat Button](https://basecoatui.com/components/button/) | `.btn` | `.btn-secondary` | `.btn-ghost` | `.btn-destructive` |\n[Kelp UI Button](https://kelpui.com/docs/components/buttons/) | `.primary` | `.secondary.muted` | `.plain` | `.danger.plain` |\n[Nuxt UI Button](https://ui.nuxt.com/docs/components/button) | `color=\"primary\" variant=\"solid\"` | `color=\"secondary\" variant=\"outline\"` | `color=\"neutral\" variant=\"ghost\"` | `color=\"error\" variant=\"ghost\"` |\n[Pico CSS button](https://picocss.com/docs/button) | - | `.secondary` | `.outline` | N/A |\n\n\n### Why standardize this?\n\nWhy standardize this? Why not just continue to do this in userland? Especially since there is no corresponding ARIA (yet)?\n\nSome reasons:\n- UAs can still hook into this with default styles and optimized interaction\n- Gives authors and tooling a standard styling hook instead of having to invent ad hoc conventions and encourages structuring design systems around semantic purpose rather than presentation\n- Encourages good UX practices with less author effort\n- Reduces motivation to recreate buttons just to improve DX\n- Its value as a standardized styling hook is motivating enough to get authors to use it, which means that down the line it can be enhanced with additional (non-breaking) improvements, e.g. if ARIA for this becomes available, it can hook into existing usage immediately\n\n## Strawman\n\nAs a strawman, I'd propose:\n- `priority=\"higher | normal | lower\"`\n- `hint=\"destructive\"`\n\nRationale / open questions:\n- Two attributes seem like a clearer model, one for priority and one for other hints (destructiveness etc). If there is less consensus for one of the two, the other is still useful.\n- What other hints may we end up having? If we can't think of any, would a boolean attribute like `destructive` make sense?\n- While many design systems treat the default case as primary, I think that secondary being the default works better for the web platform:\n  - it fits the current default state of buttons better\n  - the new attribute now symmetrically increases or decreases priority, which lends itself to nicer syntax",
